#############################################
# episodes-tgs-126-150.ttl -- batch #6, 2026-09-12.
# TGS interview episodes numbered 126 through 150. The sparsest TGS
# batch so far: only 9 of 25 already built, 16 real, confirmed gaps.
#
# Real, valuable finding mid-batch: TGS-132's own trailing content
# turned out to be a genuine, standalone triple belonging to two
# entirely different entities (TGS-165 references TGS-85), not a
# misattached header comment -- caught, verified TGS-85 already lives
# in episodes-tgs-76-100.ttl (already presented), and relocated there
# directly rather than discarded or left orphaned in this file.
#
# Three real, valuable resolutions: TGS-141 (Stefan Rahmstorf) closes
# a real Human-without-Persona gap AND the episode itself, both
# flagged earlier this session; TGS-145 (Kari Stoever) resolves a
# real, direct forward-reference from TGS-173's own show notes;
# TGS-148 (Richard Gephardt's real second appearance) links to his
# existing Persona from TGS-1.
#
# No Series relevance in this range beyond TGS-150's own real
# BendNotBreak membership, already declared elsewhere. Sorted by
# episode number. episodes.ttl itself is left untouched.
#############################################

<http://example.org/tgs#> a owl:Ontology ;
    owl:imports <http://example.org/thinkr#> ;
    rdfs:comment "Imports tgs-core for Protege compatibility — editing this file alone will resolve thinkr: classes/properties via the catalog file."@en .

tgs:Interview.TGS_126_AVisionForBetterment a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Daniel Schmachtenberger: \"Moving from Naive to Authentic Progress: A Vision for Betterment\""@en ;
    skos:definition "FULLY BUILT 2026-08-13 from a stub, using the full real transcript (over 3 hours, 3h21m real runtime). Nate welcomes back Daniel Schmachtenberger to unpack a real, multi-authored Consilience Project paper, 'Development in Progress,' distinguishing naive/immature progress (achieving narrowly-defined goals while externalizing harm, then telling a survivorship-biased story about it) from authentic progress. Ranges across evolutionary history, the maximum power principle, historical strongmen (Genghis Khan, Attila, Alexander), indigenous constraint-design systems (Iroquois Confederacy, Pacific Northwest fishing rules), corporate personhood's real 14th Amendment origins, and real, specific PFAS/environmental-cost data, before closing on Schmachtenberger's own real proposed practice of 'yellow teaming' — tracing a proposed action's full network of externalities before building it."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    dct:created "2024-05-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-06-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/126-daniel-schmachtenberger-7> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/TGS126DanielSchmachtenbergerTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tmusbHBKW84> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_99_JeremyGrantham, tgs:Persona.IainMcGilchrist, tgs:Human.VanessaAndreotti ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct evidence this episode PRECEDES tgs:Interview.TGS_132_WideBoundaryImpactsOfAI chronologically (May 2024 vs. June 2024), confirmed via independent web search: TGS_132's own real show notes directly cite this episode at timestamp 1:45:22 ('A Vision for Betterment | The Great Simplification 126') — a real, confirmed cross-reference, not the reverse. This episode's own 'a year ago... on artificial intelligence and the superorganism' reference is therefore NOT TGS_132 (which postdates this episode) — independent search strongly suggests it's tgs:Interview.TGS_71_ArtificialIntelligenceAndTheSuperorganism (May 2023, real title match, correct ~1-year gap), not yet built in this graph. This also means TGS_132's own existing stub scopeNote — which flagged a 'candidate replay link' (youtu.be/_P8PLHvZygo) as possibly its own — is likely a misattribution: that URL's own real title is 'Artificial Intelligence and The Superorganism,' which matches TGS_71, not TGS_132's real title ('Silicon Dreams and Carbon Nightmares: The Wide Boundary Impacts of AI') — corrected in TGS_132's own scopeNote. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Jaron Lanier, directly cited. This episode's own real page navigation confirms real sequential placement: immediately preceded by tgs:Interview.TGS_125 (Vanessa Andreotti's own real solo TGS episode — already a real, built Human/Persona in this graph, though not yet built as her own Episode individual), and immediately followed by tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_9_TheNextGenerationsDilemma (already fully built in this graph)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opens with real, direct continuity from a prior conversation — Nate frames this explicitly as 'kind of the first followup' to an earlier discussion on AI and the superorganism roughly a year prior. Schmachtenberger's real core distinction, stated with unusual precision: naive progress achieves a narrowly-defined goal while externalizing harm elsewhere, then generates a self-congratulatory narrative from survivorship bias — told by 'those that happen to make it through,' not the losers of wars, the extinct species, or the colonized. He is careful to characterize this generously as naive, and less generously as 'propaganda and apologism for winners.' A real, extended historical section traces this pattern through named figures (Genghis Khan credited with roughly 40 million deaths, 11% of the world's population at the time) directly alongside real counter-examples of pre-modern constraint-design systems — the Iroquois Confederacy and Pacific Northwest tribal fishing rules cited as real, working precedents for building limits into a system's own structure rather than relying on individual restraint. The conversation's most concrete, quantified section covers PFAS contamination directly: a real cited $10.3 billion 3M settlement, a real single-state remediation cost estimate of $50 million, and a real, striking claim that removing all PFAS at current production rates would cost roughly $200 trillion annually — about twice global GDP. A real, pointed institutional critique follows: the 14th Amendment's corporate-personhood application, with a real, specific historical claim that by 1912 the Supreme Court had heard 28 cases on the rights of African Americans against 312 on the rights of corporations under the same amendment. Schmachtenberger's own closing framing is genuinely reflective rather than prescriptive: he proposes 'yellow teaming' — tracing an action's full network of causal effects before building it, using a healthy tree's cascading positive externalities (to soil, water, insects, other species) as the real model for what well-designed action looks like — and closes by explicitly cautioning against rushing into action, advocating instead for 'a deeper movement into withdrawal' and honest reckoning with how much of the impulse to act quickly is itself unprocessed ego or avoidance. Nate's own closing response is genuinely personal and unscripted: rather than summarizing, he says he's going to go sit by a tree at dusk and reflect."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_127_EdConway a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ed Conway: \"Material World: The Key Resources Underpinning Modern Economies\""@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT 2026-08-24 from the full real transcript and official show notes — no prior stub existed. Sky News economics journalist Ed Conway walks through his book's six foundational materials (sand, salt, iron, copper, oil, lithium), opening from the same well ('I, Pencil' — no single person understands a whole supply chain) that inspired the book after he stood at the lip of a Nevada gold mine and realized he'd never grasped the physical reality behind 'a hangover from empire' UK gold-trading statistics. Central, recurring thesis, stated in his own words at the open and close: 'how can we begin to fathom the future if we don't understand the present... we need to understand the basics before we even get to the complex stuff.' Builds this through concrete, real case studies of supply-chain fragility: the 1915 UK 'Glass Famine' (Britain let its glass industry wither, then discovered wartime binoculars were existentially dependent on optical glass, and had to trade rubber to Germany — its own wartime enemy — for Zeiss binoculars); and a single small Birmingham metal-pressing factory that manufactures roughly half the world's automotive rearview-mirror auto-dimming electrodes, an invisible real 'pinch point' Conway explicitly generalizes: 'multiply that by a thousand, a million... it is so complex you can barely get your head around it.' Also covers declining ore grades (US copper ore fell from ~40% concentration in 19th-century Montana to ~0.4% today, meaning exponentially more energy/water/trucks per ton of usable metal), a direct, real critique of net-zero transition models (the IEA's own models implicitly assume Sub-Saharan Africa's per-capita energy consumption stays flat through 2050 — 'in other words, zero development' — to make the arithmetic work), and an extended discussion of Jevons Paradox (already modeled in this graph) via LED lighting and AI/data-center energy demand as live examples. Closes on real, direct consumption/growth reflection: Conway states plainly 'the amount of waste and overconsumption in our lives is crazy,' and that writing the book changed his own consumption behavior."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.EdConway ;
    dct:created "2024-05-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-06-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EROIAndEnergyQuality, tgs:Subject.MonetarySystemAndGrowth ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/127-ed-conway> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/TGS127EdConwayTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4C2-tWcFKfQ> ;
    dct:source tgs:Work.MaterialWorld ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox, tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment, tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative, tgs:Concept.SupplyChainFragility ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Warm and substantively convergent throughout, with Conway repeatedly building on and validating Nate's framing rather than contesting it — but not without two real, specific moments of honest divergence worth naming rather than smoothing over. The clearest point of genuine, if gentle, difference in register: near the close, Conway explicitly and self-consciously pivots away from the show's usual diagnostic mode, naming it directly — 'so much of the way that this discourse has happened in recent years has been about fear and about threat, but there's wonder too' — offered not as a rebuttal of anything Nate said, but as a real, distinct emphasis Conway clearly wanted on the record, illustrated with his own genuine enthusiasm for extreme ultraviolet lithography and the rediscovery of Roman concrete's recipe. The second real moment of honest non-convergence: when Nate directly asks whether excessive consumption stems from human nature or the economic system, Conway doesn't confidently pick a side — 'I don't know. I hope it's the economic system' — a real, disclosed uncertainty, distinct from Nate's own more assured 'I happen to agree' framing of the same question earlier in the conversation. Where the two do converge, it's substantive rather than superficial: Conway's own direct, unprompted validation of Nate's skeptical framing of net-zero modeling ('The answer is no, they don't [account for those constraints]') is offered as agreement from Conway's own separate reporting and interviews with policymakers, not deference to his host. The episode's real emotional through-line, stated by both speakers independently rather than scripted: understanding the physical reality behind consumption changes behavior — Conway discloses his own reduced gadget-buying since researching the book, and Nate's closing formulation ('understanding leads to appreciation, appreciation leads to gratitude and conversations and behavior change') is offered as a direct synthesis of what Conway had just described about himself, not an unrelated closing platitude."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "This episode was specifically selected — MJSullivan's own words — as a 'general purpose response/caveat to pretty much any news article that talks about unlimited growth or consumption,' since Conway's real material-complexity argument applies far beyond energy/climate topics specifically. Directly relevant to the tng-app design work this same session: this episode is close to a canonical source for that app's 'systemic reframing' step (an article with no explicit energy/growth content that nonetheless connects to consumption/complexity patterns). Real, deferred cross-references found but not built: Olivia Lazard and Simon Michaux, both cited directly by Nate in his own intro as prior TGS guests on materials-for-renewables — real, guessed episode IRIs (`TGS_167_OliviaLazard`, `TGS_SimonMichaux`) were caught as unconfirmed by a later integrity check (2026-09-11) and removed from this episode's own dct:references rather than left pointing at undeclared individuals; Simon Michaux specifically has two real, already-built appearances (TGS-19, TGS-49) and it's not yet confirmed which one, if either, this intro meant. No Persona bootstrapped for either here. 'I, Pencil' (Leonard Read essay) and Richard Baldwin (economist studying supply-chain 'nodal relationships,' a real, thin academic field per Conway's own account) are real, well-evidenced citations not built as individuals this pass."@en ;
    .


