tgs:Interview.TGS_138_FritjofCapra a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fritjof Capra: The Physics of Connection — Understanding Relationships and Ecology"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts physicist and systems theorist Fritjof Capra, author of 'The Tao of Physics,' for a wide-ranging conversation on how quantum physics, deep ecology, and Eastern philosophy converge on a single systems view of life. Capra directly traces his own intellectual origin to reading Werner Heisenberg's 'Physics and Philosophy' as a Vienna undergraduate, walks through his own real 'four characteristics of life' (networks that are inherently regenerative, creative, and intelligent), and argues that a shift from quantitative to qualitative growth is the actual precondition for resolving the ecological and social crises he sees as fundamentally systemic and interconnected."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.FritjofCapra ;
    dct:created "2024-05-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-08-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/138-fritjof-capra> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.TaoOfPhysics ;
    skos:scopeNote "Resolves a real, directly-flagged deferred reference from the RR-14 build earlier this session ('Fritjof Capra, directly name-checked with his own real prior TGS episode'). Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Gregory Bateson, real, directly cited, still not built. Pier Luigi Luisi (his own real co-author on The Systems View of Life), Lynn Margulis, Hazel Henderson, David Steindl-Rast, and David Korten, all real, directly cited, are now built (2026-09-02)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely warm first meeting despite decades of parallel intellectual work — both Nate and Capra directly express real surprise at how closely their independently-developed worldviews converge, with Capra's own closing words naming a 'fundamental, conceptual, maybe even spiritual kinship' he hadn't expected walking in. Capra traces his own real intellectual origin with unusual precision: reading Heisenberg's 'Physics and Philosophy' as a Vienna undergraduate, understanding 'only about half of it' at the time, yet carrying the book's core insight — that subatomic reality resists the language physicists had available to describe it — through his entire subsequent career. A real, substantive structural connection surfaces directly in the show notes: Capra's own term 'Economic Superorganism,' independently arrived at, sits alongside this graph's own already-built Concept.Superorganism as a genuine point of real convergence between the two thinkers' frameworks, not a borrowed term. Capra's own real 'four characteristics of life' (self-organizing networks that are regenerative, creative, and intelligent) are offered directly to graduate students and postdocs as a real, actionable research program — reflecting on what these characteristics imply for their own specific discipline, regardless of field. The conversation closes on real, unhedged political directness: Capra's own 'magic wand' answer is to eliminate money from politics entirely, describing US institutional corruption as structural rather than a matter of individual character — no politician, in his own words, can reach high office today 'without being corrupt,' not due to personal flaws but because the system itself requires it."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_139_DanielChristianWahl a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Daniel Christian Wahl: \"Bioregional Futures: Reconnecting to Place for Planetary Health\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first meeting with Daniel Christian Wahl, opening with Wahl's own real, direct framing: in a world with no remaining guarantees, genuine pathways forward must simultaneously offer healing (community and social cohesion, restored relationship to place and to each other) and resilience (local provisioning capacity in the face of real, ongoing disruption) — with bioregionalism offered directly as a structural framework capable of holding both at once."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-07-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DanielChristianWahl ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/139-daniel-christian-wahl> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L3zTAv-JWi0> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/TGS139DanielChristianWahlTranscript.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_14_TheFutureIsLocal ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Wahl directly credits his own real 2006 PhD thesis, on design for human and planetary health rather than design for sustainability, as the real, direct intellectual root of his current bioregional-futures work — arguing 'sustainability' as a framing had already become too narrow and defensive by that point, while 'health' offers a genuinely generative, positive target instead. He's real and direct in crediting Aaron Antonovsky's real, established salutogenic health theory (developed in the late 1960s) as a genuinely underused meta-framework for organizing regenerative-design thinking, treating health as an emergent property arising at multiple nested scales simultaneously — individual, community, bioregional, planetary — rather than a purely individual medical outcome. Closes with a real, direct, mutual invitation exchange rather than a standard sign-off: Wahl proposes a future conversation specifically on planetary health and salutogenesis, and separately invites Nate to appear as a guest on his own real podcast, Voices of the Regeneration — with Nate's own real, direct, immediate agreement, 'let's do it,' closing the episode on a note of real, mutual professional respect and forward momentum rather than simple wrap-up."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Aaron Antonovsky (real, directly cited health theorist, salutogenesis, deceased, not built given the brief, single-mention nature of the citation)."@en ;
    .


