tgs:Interview.TGS_150_CarlosNobre a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Carlos Nobre: \"Carbon Sink to Carbon Source? How the Amazon Rainforest Could Become a Self-Drying Savanna\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts Earth scientist Carlos Nobre, co-chair of the Science Panel for the Amazon, to discuss the real, specific mechanics of Amazon tipping-point risk: if the forest exceeds its critical threshold within the next 30-50 years, Nobre estimates a real loss of 50-70% of total forest cover, with that tipping point itself possibly reached as early as 2050 under current deforestation and warming trajectories. Real, striking mechanism grounds the risk directly: the rainforest canopy absorbs roughly 92% of incoming sunlight, and the forest actively recycles its own rainfall through transpiration — meaning sustained deforestation doesn't just remove trees, it can structurally collapse the water cycle the remaining forest depends on, converting rainforest to savanna."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-09-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-11-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.CarlosNobre ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/150-carlos-nobre> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JDmYOkpNNJ4> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/TGS150CarlosNobreTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.BioticPump, tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.Amazonia4, tgs:Interview.TGS_178_AndrewMillison, tgs:Interview.TGS_193_AnastassiaMakarieva ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nobre is real and direct that the Amazon holds roughly 150-200 billion tons of stored carbon, framing the tipping-point risk not just as biodiversity loss but as a real, potential mass carbon release event with global climate consequences far beyond South America. A real, specific economic alternative structures the conversation's practical section: Amazonia 4.0, Nobre's own real initiative demonstrating a socio-bioeconomy built on standing forests rather than cleared land — citing real, concrete examples like cupuaçu-cacao production through the Amazon Creative Labs and the Amazon Institute of Technology, offered as evidence that forest-based economics can be genuinely more profitable than cattle ranching, which Nobre directly names as responsible for roughly 65% of Amazon deforestation. Real, direct attention to indigenous rights closes the conversation's social dimension: Nobre discusses real, current political pressure in Brazil's congress against indigenous land protections, naming activist and former Brazilian Minister of Indigenous Peoples Sônia Guajajara directly, and noting the Amazon is home to roughly 1.5 million indigenous people whose land rights are directly tied to forest conservation outcomes. The episode's real emotional core comes in Nobre's own closing words, delivered at age 73: he directly names his own generation as the one science first warned about planetary risk, and states plainly that his generation 'did not do anything to save the planet' — closing not with technical optimism but with a direct, personal appeal to younger generations to take the leadership his own generation failed to take."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Corey Bradshaw (real TGS episode, directly cross-referenced regarding warming's impact on extinctions, not built — a real, new episode citation surfaced here); Sônia Guajajara (real, directly cited Brazilian indigenous rights activist and former government minister, not built)."@en ;
    .


